{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.MathlibExt.ExponentialGrowthRate","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.ExponentialGrowthRate","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.SpecialFunctions.Log.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.SpecialFunctions.Pow.Real","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.SpecialFunctions.Pow.Asymptotics","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.Complex.Trigonometric","lean:module:Mathlib.Topology.Algebra.Order.Field"],"declarations":7,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.tendsto_log_div_of_sandwich_const","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.tendsto_log_div_of_sandwich_const","kind":"theorem","line":210,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/ExponentialGrowthRate.lean#L210-L219","doc":"The constant-factor case: a bound that costs only a fixed factor — an oscillatory\nsum kept inside a half-plane, say — costs nothing at all in the growth rate. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.tendsto_log_div_of_sandwich","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.tendsto_log_div_of_sandwich","kind":"theorem","line":171,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/ExponentialGrowthRate.lean#L171-L208","doc":"**A sub-exponential sandwich does not move the growth rate.**\n\nIf `m N ≤ S N ≤ c N · m N` with `m` positive and `log (c N)/N → 0`, then `S` and `m`\nhave the same exponential growth rate.\n\nThe hypotheses are stated `∀ᶠ` rather than `∀` because the interesting instances only\nsandwich for large `N` — a Kashaev sum has no useful bound at `N = 2`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.tendsto_log_norm_div_of_tendsto_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.tendsto_log_norm_div_of_tendsto_one","kind":"theorem","line":125,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/ExponentialGrowthRate.lean#L125-L155","doc":"**The statement a volume-conjecture bridge consumes.**\n\nFrom the asymptotic expansion alone — no steepest descent, no geometry — the\nexponential growth rate of `f` is `Re ζ`.\n\nThis is part (C) of `QOU.Archimedean.FiveTwoOhtsukiBridge`, stated once, for any knot\nwhose Kashaev invariant has such an expansion. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.tendsto_log_norm_model","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.tendsto_log_norm_model","kind":"theorem","line":83,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/ExponentialGrowthRate.lean#L83-L123","doc":"**The model's growth rate is `Re ζ`.**\n\n`‖e^{Nζ}·N^p·ω‖ = e^{N·Re ζ}·N^p·‖ω‖`, so its log over `N` is\n`Re ζ + p·(log N)/N + log‖ω‖/N`, and both correction terms vanish. The exponent `p` is\narbitrary — `3/2` for Ohtsuki's `5₂`, `1/2` for the deformed torus-knot expansions —\nand never enters the limit. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.tendsto_log_norm_sub_div_atTop","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.tendsto_log_norm_sub_div_atTop","kind":"theorem","line":64,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/ExponentialGrowthRate.lean#L64-L81","doc":"**Asymptotic equivalence preserves the exponential growth rate.**\n\nIf `f N / g N → 1` then `(log‖f N‖ − log‖g N‖)/N → 0`. The `(1 + o(1))` factor of an\nasymptotic expansion is invisible at this resolution — which is the entire reason a\nvolume conjecture can be read off an expansion whose subleading terms nobody has\ncomputed. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.tendsto_const_div_natCast_atTop","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.tendsto_const_div_natCast_atTop","kind":"theorem","line":59,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/ExponentialGrowthRate.lean#L59-L62","doc":"`c / N → 0` along `ℕ`, for a constant `c`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.tendsto_log_natCast_div_atTop","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.tendsto_log_natCast_div_atTop","kind":"theorem","line":54,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/ExponentialGrowthRate.lean#L54-L57","doc":"`log N / N → 0` along `ℕ`. The one place `N^p` is shown to be sub-exponential. ","signatureChunk":0}]}